From ed1c0b1fbb4cb68abffcb6250cef4c5d38e67ae2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabriel Fernandez <gabriel.fernandez@foss.st.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 08:26:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: refresh the capacity reference frequency on boost
 changes

capacity_freq_ref is frozen at CPUFREQ_CREATE_POLICY to the non-boost
policy->cpuinfo.max_freq. It is read through arch_scale_freq_ref() by both
the frequency invariance engine (as the scaling denominator) and the
schedutil governor (get_capacity_ref_freq()).

On platforms using software boost (cpufreq_boost_set_sw), such as STM32MP2
with the SCMI cpufreq driver, the boost state is toggled at runtime and
cpufreq_frequency_table_cpuinfo() recomputes policy->cpuinfo.max_freq to
include or exclude the boost frequencies, but capacity_freq_ref is never
refreshed. As a result schedutil keeps computing:

    raw = map_util_freq(util, ref, max) = ref * util / max

with ref pinned to the non-boost maximum and util bounded by max, so the
requested frequency can never exceed the non-boost maximum and a boost OPP
is unreachable regardless of load. The ondemand and performance governors
are unaffected because they target policy->max directly.

Call arch_set_freq_ref() from cpufreq_boost_set_sw() right after
cpuinfo.max_freq has been recomputed, keeping the reference in sync with
the reachable maximum. The reference is only raised while boost is enabled,
so the capacity model is unchanged when boost is off.

Assisted-by: GitHub-Copilot:Claude-Opus-4.8
Signed-off-by: Patrick Delaunay <patrick.delaunay@foss.st.com>
Signed-off-by: Gabriel Fernandez <gabriel.fernandez@foss.st.com>
Change-Id: Ia7eb3d199406926877e15c04d7c6f802fe20a367
Reviewed-on: https://gerrit.st.com/c/mpu/oe/st/linux-stm32/+/592687
ACI: CIBUILD <MDG-smet-aci-builds@list.st.com>
---
 drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq.c | 7 +++++++
 include/linux/cpufreq.h   | 7 +++++++
 2 files changed, 14 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq.c
@@ -2793,6 +2793,13 @@ int cpufreq_boost_set_sw(struct cpufreq_
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Keep the capacity reference frequency in sync with the reachable
+	 * maximum so that frequency invariance and the schedutil governor can
+	 * account for boost frequencies entering or leaving cpuinfo.max_freq.
+	 */
+	arch_set_freq_ref(policy->related_cpus, policy->cpuinfo.max_freq);
+
 	ret = freq_qos_update_request(policy->max_freq_req, policy->max);
 	if (ret < 0)
 		return ret;
--- a/include/linux/cpufreq.h
+++ b/include/linux/cpufreq.h
@@ -1224,6 +1224,13 @@ void arch_set_freq_scale(const struct cp
 }
 #endif
 
+#ifndef arch_set_freq_ref
+static __always_inline
+void arch_set_freq_ref(const struct cpumask *cpus, unsigned long max_freq)
+{
+}
+#endif
+
 /* the following are really really optional */
 extern struct freq_attr cpufreq_freq_attr_scaling_available_freqs;
 extern struct freq_attr cpufreq_freq_attr_scaling_boost_freqs;
